<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the notarized Clew.app — the `clew` GUI
  binary and the bundle. Deliberately EMPTY: the hardened runtime applies in
  full to the process that holds the user's API keys and approvals.

  Clew is not App-Sandboxed (it is a developer tool that reads arbitrary local
  projects and spawns external toolchains), so no sandbox or network
  entitlements are declared either.

  What used to be here, and why it is gone:
    - com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit and
      com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory were claimed for
      the wgpu/Metal renderer. Metal compiles shaders in Apple's own compiler
      service, out of process; clew never maps writable executable memory.
    - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation was claimed for the
      language servers and debug adapters clew downloads. Library validation
      governs which dylibs THIS process may load, not which programs it may
      spawn: those run as separate processes under their own signatures, and
      clew loads no third-party dylibs.
  Each one weakened a protection for nothing it actually needed.

  clew-server is signed with the hardened runtime and NO entitlements at all
  (.github/workflows/release.yml); it must never be given any of these.

  Re-adding an entitlement takes a reproduced failure under the hardened
  runtime (for example a future renderer that JIT-compiles in process), the
  narrowest key that fixes it (allow-jit before
  allow-unsigned-executable-memory), and a line here naming the component
  that needs it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
